The given differential equation is
$\frac{d y}{d x}=(x-y)^2\begin{array}{ll}\text { Put } & x-y=t \\ \Rightarrow & 1-\frac{d y}{d x}=\frac{d t}{d x} \\ \Rightarrow & \frac{d y}{d x}=1-\frac{d t}{d x}\end{array}
$\begin{array}{ll}
\therefore & 1-\frac{d t}{d x}=t^2 \\
\Rightarrow & \left(1-t^2\right)=\frac{d t}{d x}
\end{array}
On integrating both sides, we get
$\begin{array}{rlrl}
\int \frac{1}{1-t^2} d t & =\int 1 d x \\
\Rightarrow \quad & \frac{1}{2} \log \left(\frac{1+t}{1-t}\right) & =x+c \\
\Rightarrow \quad\left(\frac{1+t}{1-t}\right) & =e^{2 x+2 c} \\
\Rightarrow \quad \frac{1+x-y}{1-x+y} & =A e^{2 x}
\end{array}
$\because$ It passes through origin.
$\therefore \quad A=1\therefore$ Required curve is
$(1+x-y)=e^{2 x}(1-x+y)

Given, $y=A x^2+B x$....(i)<br/>On differentiating, we get
$\begin{array}{rlrl} 
& \frac{d y}{d x} & =2 A x+B \\
\Rightarrow & & \frac{d^2 y}{d x^2} & =2 A
\end{array}
$\therefore \quad \frac{d y}{d x}-\frac{d^2 y}{d x^2} x=B$ \\ From Eq. (i),
$\begin{array}{ll} 
& y=\frac{1}{2} \frac{d^2 y}{d x^2} x^2+x\left[\frac{d y}{d x}-\frac{d^2 y}{d x^2} x\right] \\
\Rightarrow & y=-\frac{1}{2} x^2 \frac{d^2 y}{d x^2}+x \frac{d y}{d x} \\
\therefore \quad & x^2 \frac{d^2 y}{d x^2}-2 x \frac{d y}{d x}+2 y=0
\end{array}

We have,
$x=e^{x y \frac{d y}{d x}}\begin{array}{ll}\Rightarrow & \log x=x y \frac{d y}{d x} \\ \Rightarrow & y d y=\frac{\log x}{x} d x \\ \Rightarrow & y d y=\log x d(\log x)\end{array}
On integrating both sides, we get
$\frac{y^2}{2}=\frac{\left(\log _\theta x\right)^2}{2}+C\begin{array}{ll}\Rightarrow & y^2=\left(\log _\theta x\right)^2+2 C \\ \Rightarrow & y= \pm \sqrt{\left(\log _\theta x\right)^2+2 C}\end{array}
